==English==
{{slim-wikipedia|Mass (disambiguation)}}
[[File:Frog egg mass.jpg|right|thumb|160px|A mass (aggregation) of frog eggs]]

===Etymology 1===
In late {{inh|en|enm|-}} (circa 1400) as {{m|enm|masse}} in the sense of "lump, quantity of matter", from {{der|en|xno|masse}}, in {{der|en|fro|-}} attested from the 11th century, via late {{der|en|la|massa||lump, dough}}, from {{der|en|grc|μᾶζα|t=barley-cake, lump (of dough)}}. The Greek noun may be derived from the verb {{m|grc|μάσσω||to knead}}, ultimately from a {{der|en|ine-pro|*maǵ-|t=to oil, knead}}, although this is uncertain.<ref>Beekes, Etymological dictionary of Greek</ref> {{doublet|en|masa}}.
The sense of "a large number or quantity" arises circa 1580. The scientific sense is from 1687 (as Latin {{m|la|massa}}) in the works of [[w:Sir Isaac Newton|Isaac Newton]], with the first English use (as {{m|en|mass}}) occurring in 1704.

